 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The standard implementation involves multiple 2D convolutions, which is $O(N \cdot C \cdot H \cdot W \cdot K^2)$ where $K$ is the window size. Optimized implementations using separable 1D convolutions reduce this to $O(N \cdot C \cdot H \cdot W \cdot K)$ [S6].
+- **Time Complexity:** The standard implementation involves multiple 2D convolutions [S4, unofficial]. Optimized implementations use separable 1D convolutions to reduce computation [S6].
 - **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing intermediate tensors for means, variances, and covariance, proportional to the image size $H \times W$ [S4, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Target SSIM scores for high-quality reconstructions are typically 0.95+ [S2, unofficial]. The metric is computationally expensive compared to MSE, especially with large window sizes, but provides better correlation with human perception [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Target SSIM scores for high-quality reconstructions are typically 0.95+ [S2, unofficial].
 - **Strengths:** Captures structural information and perceived quality better than pixel-wise metrics [S1, unofficial][S3].
-- **Limitations:** Sensitive to misalignment and blur; computationally heavier than MSE [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When perceptual quality is more important than pixel-perfect accuracy, or as a regularization term in differentiable rendering to encourage structural fidelity [S2, unofficial][S6].
-- **When not to use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and MSE is sufficient for convergence, or when strict pixel alignment cannot be guaranteed [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** The main downside is its complexity and the nonlinearity of the human visual system [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** Used as a loss term in differentiable rendering frameworks like 3D Gaussian Splatting to encourage structural fidelity [S6].
 
 ## Background
-The Structural Similarity Index was proposed by Wang et al. in 2004 [S1, S2, unofficial]. It was designed to address the limitations of traditional metrics like MSE and PSNR by modeling image distortion as a change in structural information, based on the assumption that the human visual system is highly adapted to structured natural scene information [S1, unofficial]. It has since become a standard benchmark for image quality assessment in computer vision and graphics [S2, unofficial][S3].
